## Why turn-taking is important

Deciding when to take a turn is a crucial part of any speech-to-speech pipeline. Currently there are two approaches for building such pipelines – using a cascade of different models or picking a single unified <Term tip="The model listens and speaks at the same time, like a phone call, instead of taking strict turns like a walkie-talkie.">full-duplex</Term> model. Unified models, such as Moshi,<Cite id="moshi" /> are more natural conversationalists – they can handle speech overlaps and backchannels ("mm-hmm", "yeah", etc.), but they are difficult to train, requiring rare speech-to-speech data. The cascaded approach, on the other hand, is more flexible – you usually pick three models – an <Term tip="Automatic speech recognition: turns the user's speech into text.">ASR</Term> model, an <Term tip="Large language model: reads the transcript and writes the text of the reply.">LLM</Term>, and a <Term tip="Text-to-speech: turns the reply text back into audio.">TTS</Term> – and have a ready speech-to-speech pipeline in no time. Little to no training is required, and individual components can be replaced with newer ones as they become available. However, such cascaded approaches have two downsides – they usually suffer from higher latency (200 ms for Moshi compared to \~1 second of a cascaded pipeline<Cite id="chipchat" note="920 ms of latency was achieved on a highly-optimized pipeline on an Apple M2 Ultra." />) and they do not sound as natural in the conversation, since a lot of expressiveness is lost on the switch between modalities – the STT→LLM transition, for example, loses everything but text. Let's have a look at some examples:

<ConversationDemo
  demo="moshi"
  title="Unified (Moshi)"
  description="Note the rapid response time and the ability to handle backchannels."
/>

<ConversationDemo
  demo="pipecat"
  title="Cascaded (Pipecat)"
  description="Here, the latency is more substantial, but the model is way smarter."
/>

<p class="demo-notes">
  Both were run on an RTX 4090. The cascaded approach is running on Voxtral Realtime, Gemma 4 12B and Pocket TTS. The cascaded approach provides more thoughful answers, has a better TTS and recognizes words better, but lacks interactivity – notice the interrupt on the "yeah" backchannel. For benchmarks on Moshi and how it fares against other models, visit <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/speech-to-speech" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Artificial Analysis</a>.
</p>



We are focused on perfecting the cascaded approach and making it as natural as possible, so let's talk a bit more about turn-taking latency in cascades. Nowadays, the pipeline can decide when to take a turn in three ways:

1. A separate turn-taking model is used, which fires a signal for the LLM to start speaking (for example, Smart Turn<Cite id="smart-turn" />).
2. The ASR itself emits the end-of-turn signal to the LLM (X2-Turn<Cite id="x2-turn" /> and Kyutai STT<Cite id="kyutai-stt" />).
3. ASR tokens are streamed to the LLM, and it decides on a fixed timer whether to start talking or keep silent – the DuplexCascade approach.<Cite id="duplexcascade" note="A decision on whether to start talking or not is made every 600 ms." />

<DuplexCascade />

And in all such pipelines it is paramount to balance accuracy and speed very carefully – take a turn too quickly while the user is thinking mid-sentence, and your user gets furious that he was interrupted mid-thought by something like "It looks like your response was cut off, what did you mean?". Take a turn too slowly and you get unnatural response times, again, ruining user experience. Humans usually give replies in about 200 ms in a natural conversation.<Cite id="levinson-torreira" />

## Existing solutions

Now that we know what turn-taking is, let's take a look at existing solutions. A popular benchmark for turn-taking models is [TurnBench](https://turnbench.sesame.com/) – we picked the open-source entries, added other turn detectors and a plain silence timeout, and tested them all on our data:

**Turn-end detection on real speakrail conversations**

| Model | Turn ends caught\* | Response delay<br />(typical / slowest 10%) | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| VoxTurn | 94% | 0.08 s / 0.4 s | CC-BY-NC |
| MaAI VAP | 91% | 0.3 s / 1.0 s | MIT code; weights' licence not stated |
| Smart Turn v3.2 | 91% | 0.8 s / 1.2 s | BSD-2 |
| Silence timeout (Silero VAD) | 89% | 2.2 s / 2.3 s | MIT |
| X2-Turn | 74% | 1.4 s / 3.3 s | Apache 2.0 (+ Mistral terms) |
| turn-1-mini | 67% | 0.16 s / 1.2 s | Apache 2.0 |
| LiveKit turn detector (text) | 66% | \~0.2 s\*\* | LiveKit model licence |
| Parakeet realtime EOU | 58% | 2.2 s / 5.8 s | NVIDIA Open Model |
| TEN Turn Detection (text) | 50% | \~0.2 s\*\* | Apache 2.0 |

<p class="table-notes">
  * Share of finished turns the model responds to, while interrupting the user on at most 5% of mid-sentence pauses. Delay is measured from the user’s last word.<br />
  ** Text-only models were given an assumed 0.2 s for the transcript to arrive.
</p>

## Our approach

Our main idea was to train a turn-taking head to an ASR model to decide when to release turns – this allows us to tap into the powerful acoustic and semantic features that the model was previously trained on. As our ASR we chose the Voxtral Realtime<Cite id="voxtral-realtime" /> model, since it shows the best performance among all open-source streaming models we found, both on [public tests](https://artificialanalysis.ai/speech-to-text/streaming) and on our benchmarks. Here are the results of our benchmarks:

**Errors on real speakrail conversations**

| System | Meaning-changing errors per turn\* | How it ran |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Voxtral Realtime** | **5.3%** | live streaming, 480 ms delay |
| Qwen3-ASR 1.7B | 8.1% | offline |
| Whisper large-v3 | 12.0% | offline |
| Parakeet TDT v3 | 14.3% | offline |
| Nemotron streaming-en 0.6B | 16.9% | streaming, 560 ms |
| Kyutai STT 2.6B | 21.5% | offline |
| Nemotron 3.5 0.6B | 27.3% | streaming, 560 ms |
| Kyutai STT 1B | 29.6% | offline |
| Moonshine v2 Medium (245M) | 34.2% | offline, whole clips, batched |
| Gemma 4 audio (E4B / E2B / 12B) | 40–44% | offline |

<p class="table-notes">
  * Meaning-changing errors per turn is an LLM judged-metric that inspects the transcripts and decides whether the meaning was altered or not. Claude Opus 5.5 was used as a judge.
</p>

Good, the ASR model is selected, now we need to decide how exactly a turn-taking head should be attached to the model. **Our hypothesis: since the Voxtral model is capable of outputting punctuation, it should have an idea of <Term tip="The melody and rhythm of speech: pitch, loudness, pauses and timing. It is how a listener hears that a sentence is finished, or isn't.">prosody</Term> and semantics, so, in theory, weights can be frozen and a turn-taking head can be attached directly onto the hidden states and trained to get information from them.** Keeping weights frozen would keep <Term tip="Word error rate: the share of words an ASR model gets wrong (swapped, dropped or added) against a reference transcript. Lower is better.">WER</Term> as is, and ensure that the model doesn't catastrophically forget everything that it learned before – it was never trained to output turn-taking probabilities after all. Furthermore, such an approach allows us to use datasets that don't have text as output (Smart Turn v3.2, for example). Another hypothesis is that the most useful information would lie somewhere in the middle of the decoder: in decoder-only language models, intermediate layers often carry richer representations than the last one, which specializes in predicting the next token.<Cite id="skean" note="Across decoder-only language models, intermediate layers consistently give stronger features for downstream tasks than the final layer." /> Layers closer to the input, in turn, stay closer to the acoustics.<Cite id="pasad" note="In wav2vec 2.0, information climbs from acoustic features in the lowest layers to phones, word identity and word meaning higher up." />

<DecoderTaps />

Let's check our hypotheses, starting with the place to tap into. We picked 2,000 clips from Smart Turn at random, saved decoder outputs from multiple layers and then trained small linear probes that output simple complete / incomplete probabilities. Complete probability means that the phrase is complete and that we can ask the LLM to "say" something, and incomplete means that the user has probably paused mid-sentence to think. Here are the results of this experiment:

**Where the turn-taking information lives in Voxtral's decoder**

| Decoder layer (of 26) | Accuracy, complete vs incomplete\* |
| --- | --- |
| 0 (first) | 92.5% |
| 4 | 95.3% |
| 11 (middle) | **96.4%** |
| 17 | 96.1% |
| Output (last layer, after the final norm) | 93.5% |

<p class="table-notes">
  * A linear probe per layer, trained on 1,603 clips and tested on the 397 held-out clips. Accuracy is averaged over 0.5–1 s after the user stops speaking, which is when Voxtral emits the last word.
</p>

Our hypothesis held up: the most useful information sits in the middle layers. We expected a bigger gap, honestly, but the result was clear enough, so we chose layer 11 as the input for the turn-taking head.

To choose the classifier, we took a 19,700 clip subset from the [Smart Turn v3.1 training set](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train). We ran them through a frozen Voxtral Realtime model using the transformers package and saved the hidden states of layers 11, 17 and the output layer, for the frames around the end of each utterance. Here is the comparison table on the cached hidden states:

**Candidates we tried**

| Head | Size | Reads | F1 complete / incomplete / backchannel | False fires on incomplete |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linear probe | 15k | layer 11 | 0.951 / 0.948 / 0.928 | 5.7% |
| MLP, 768 hidden | 2.4M | layer 11 | 0.963 / 0.963 / 0.944 | 5.2% |
| GRU, 512 | 6.3M | layers 11 + 17 + output | 0.971 / 0.971 / 0.954 | 2.9% |
| LoRA rank 16 on Voxtral's top layers 18–25, plus a linear head | 2.9M | layer 17 | 0.964 / 0.964 / 0.949 | 3.4% |

All classifiers show very similar performance on the TurnBench datasets, however, when we tested the performance on the CANDOR dataset, we saw that the simple MLP on layer 11 was better on catching real turn ends, missing only on backchannels.

**The same heads on CANDOR, a different domain**

| Head | CANDOR turn ends caught (119) | Typical delay | Cut in on mid-turn pauses | Took the floor on a backchannel |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linear, layer 11 | 22% | 0.23 s | 10% | 34% |
| **MLP, layer 11** | **57%** | **0.26 s** | 19% | 31% |
| GRU, layer 11 | 37% | 0.14 s | 13% | 2% |
| LoRA, top layers | 25% | 0.15 s | 10% | 0% |

After picking the layer-11 MLP, we trained the it on a subset of the OpenYap dataset and the entire SmartTurn train dataset. We ran the model on an RTX 4090, collecting the layer-11 activations, and then trained the MLP on those activations. The backchannel performance was also improved, since OpenYap had more backchannel examples than SmartTurn. After training, we got the best result on the speakrail audios:

**Turn-end detection on real speakrail conversations**

| Model | Turn ends caught\* | Response delay<br />(typical / slowest 10%) | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Speakrail Voxtral** | 96% | 0.08 s / 0.3 s | Apache 2.0 |
| VoxTurn | 94% | 0.08 s / 0.4 s | CC-BY-NC |
| MaAI VAP | 91% | 0.3 s / 1.0 s | MIT code; weights' licence not stated |
| Smart Turn v3.2 | 91% | 0.8 s / 1.2 s | BSD-2 |
| ... | ... | ... | ... |

Submission to the TurnBench table is coming soon.

### Speeding up Voxtral

Great, the model now can tell the LLM when to take a turn. However, Voxtral is very slow -- it shows the best WER tradeoff at 480ms after the end of word (source), and its end of word trails a VAD mark by another 100-200ms, raising the total delay to 680ms. Changing the latency to something less than 480ms will increase the error rate and make the model more prone to missing words. However, we can improve latency thanks to the turn-taking head we just trained. The turn-taking head outputs end-of-turn probabilities every Voxtral frame, that is 80ms, and it is not using the same delay-conditioning as the decoder does when outputting the words. This means that, knowing that a turn is over, we can push empty audio chunks and force a faster than realtime computation to cut down the end-of-turn latency. The RTX 4090 running the Voxtral model through the audio.cpp backend allows to run the model 6 times faster than realtime, which means that we can theoretically cut end of word latency from 480ms to just 80ms. Here are some Gantt charts for easier understanding of the concept:

<SpeedupGantt />

Lastly, as you can see on the Gantt charts, there is a gap between the actual end of word and when Voxtral thinks there is the end of word, so we add a small sigmoid output to the head and ask it to output probabilities that the phrase will end 80ms/160ms/240ms from the current timestamp. This idea didn't help much in terms of latency, saving only 60-70ms.

## Results


First and foremost, Voxtral retained its WER, since the weights were frozen. While using audio.cpp, the Speakrail and base Voxtral returned bit-identical outputs in both engines. The head itself costs just 0.02ms on audio.cpp and consists of just 2M parameters. Secondly, the peek functionality gets 80% of turn releases right, leaving just 20% of redundant compute for an average of 500ms of saved turn-release latency.

## Limitations and future work

First of all, more data can be used to make the pipeline more robust - there is the oto-speech dataset and there is the full set of OpenYap. Furthermore, the turn-taking head is currently most suited for assistant-like work, and in general human-like chit-chatting, it can misinterpret mid-sentence pauses and trigger a turn release. Furthermore, this approach should generalize to other frame-synchronous ASR, such as Nemotron Streaming, however this hypothesis has to be thoroughly checked because the Nemotron model doesn't have the LLM decoder that Voxtral has.


## Deployment
To test the turn-taking head in your environment, you can use our fork of the [audio.cpp](https://github.com/speakrail/audio.cpp/tree/turn-head-2611) repository together with the [HF repository](https://huggingface.co/speakrail/speakrail-voxtral-realtime-2602-turnhead-2611) for the latest trained turn-taking head checkpoint. However, you can try the Voxtral model in [Speakrail](https://github.com/speakrail/speakrail) – our open-source full-duplex speech-to-speech pipeline that uses this Voxtral model in action. Feel free to leave pull requests and open issues there.
